1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,440
Prime numbers are the atoms of arithmetic.

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:07,084
Every whole number is built by multiplying primes together.

3
00:00:07,224 --> 00:00:12,403
Yet the primes themselves seem to turn up
at random, with no pattern anyone can see.

4
00:00:13,633 --> 00:00:15,831
Step back, though, and order appears.

5
00:00:15,971 --> 00:00:19,067
Count the primes as you go, and you get a staircase.

6
00:00:19,207 --> 00:00:24,901
From far away, that staircase hugs a smooth
curve, one that Gauss guessed as a teenager.

7
00:00:25,041 --> 00:00:29,867
So here is the real question. How far
does the staircase stray from the curve?

8
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:33,859
Think of flipping a fair coin a million times.

9
00:00:33,999 --> 00:00:39,117
Heads and tails will differ by something like
a thousand, the square root of a million.

10
00:00:39,257 --> 00:00:41,514
That is the fingerprint of pure chance.

11
00:00:41,654 --> 00:00:45,948
The Riemann hypothesis says the
primes are just as well behaved.

12
00:00:46,088 --> 00:00:49,045
Their error stays on the scale of the square root of x.

13
00:00:50,267 --> 00:00:54,331
In 1859, Bernhard Riemann found a way to see this.

14
00:00:54,471 --> 00:01:00,103
He studied the primes through one function,
called zeta, which lives on the complex plane.

15
00:01:00,243 --> 00:01:03,990
Travel up a vertical line, and zeta traces a looping path.

16
00:01:04,130 --> 00:01:08,324
On one special line, the path keeps
passing exactly through zero.

17
00:01:08,464 --> 00:01:10,988
Those moments are the zeros of zeta.

18
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:14,920
And the zeros know everything about the primes.

19
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:17,266
Each zero contributes one wave.

20
00:01:17,406 --> 00:01:23,377
Add the waves together, one after another, and
the prime staircase emerges from smooth ripples.

21
00:01:23,517 --> 00:01:26,785
The zeros are the notes. The primes are the music.

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,294
Where a zero sits decides how loud its wave is.

23
00:01:31,434 --> 00:01:36,338
On the special line, every wave grows
at the same gentle, square root rate.

24
00:01:36,478 --> 00:01:43,753
A single zero off the line would make a wave that outgrows
all the others, and the balance of the primes would be lost.

25
00:01:43,893 --> 00:01:48,781
Riemann's hypothesis is that this never
happens. Every zero is on the line.

26
00:01:48,921 --> 00:01:52,520
For 167 years, nobody could prove it.

27
00:01:53,733 --> 00:01:57,974
There is a parallel world where the
same statement was proved decades ago.

28
00:01:58,114 --> 00:02:01,288
There, whole numbers are replaced by polynomials.

29
00:02:01,428 --> 00:02:07,802
One proof uses a fact from school algebra: a
polynomial cannot have more roots than its degree.

30
00:02:07,942 --> 00:02:12,963
So if every root is forced to count many
times over, there cannot be many roots.

31
00:02:13,103 --> 00:02:15,486
That counting trick delivers the square root.

32
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:19,493
For whole numbers, the trick always broke down.

33
00:02:19,633 --> 00:02:25,599
To make a root count many times, you take
derivatives. And a whole number has no derivative.

34
00:02:25,739 --> 00:02:33,253
The proof announced this month by Levent Alpöge, working
with the AI model Claude, is reported to find a substitute.

35
00:02:33,393 --> 00:02:37,074
It builds a long chain of operations called theta lifts.

36
00:02:37,214 --> 00:02:44,738
Each link adds one more layer of divisibility by primes,
doing the job that a derivative does for polynomials.

37
00:02:45,967 --> 00:02:47,380
Where does it stand today?

38
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:55,131
The proof is 17 million lines of code in a language called
Lean, and independent proof checkers have accepted it.

39
00:02:55,271 --> 00:03:01,338
But no person has yet read and understood the
whole argument. That work has only just begun.

40
00:03:01,478 --> 00:03:05,516
So for now: checked by machine,
not yet understood by people.

41
00:03:06,733 --> 00:03:11,880
If it holds up, one of the oldest questions
about numbers finally has its answer.

42
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:14,897
The primes are as orderly as randomness allows.
